    "question": "How can a visible indicator preserve unknown and released logic instead of guessing a binary value?",
    "notes": "Four scalar patterns cycle through 1, 0, X and Z at one-millisecond boundaries. Every interval contains one example of each state.\n\nExpected: Only the HIGH indicator glows red. LOW stays dark, X is explicitly unknown, and Z is explicitly high impedance. Cursor A selects the preserved event states at that exact time.\n\nTry: Run, then move Cursor A before and after 1 ms. At an event time, the newly recorded state is already in effect. Clear the cursor to return to the last retained states.",
    "assumptions": [
      "Logic indicators observe resolved 0/1/X/Z events. They add no load or drive strength, and the red glow does not represent LED current or physical brightness.",
      "The displayed state belongs to the selected time and the current project revision. Missing or stale results remain unobserved.",
      "The digital-only source output is a binary logic signal. Its optional voltage terminals must be enabled and wired explicitly for analog experiments."
    ],
